aarch64: zero-extend packed child pointers in the duct-tape priority queue

The XNU pairing-heap in osfmk/kern packs each node child pointer into a signed
long child : 48 bitfield and unpacks it by simply reading the bitfield, which
sign-extends bit 47. Upstream relies on that trick to rebuild kernel pointers
(canonical high-half 0xffff....) while keeping NULL as 0.

ciderd runs this scheduler in USERSPACE. On x86_64 that is harmless: user VA is
47-bit, so bit 47 is always 0 and the sign-extension is a no-op. On arm64 the
user VA is 48-bit and ciderd itself is mapped in the high quarter (e.g.
0xae.._....), so a valid priority_queue node can legitimately have bit 47 set.
unpack_child() then sign-extends it to a bogus 0xffff.... pointer and meld_pair()
dereferences it -> SIGSEGV (ciderd: FATAL host signal 11, fault addr like
0xffffae103f9f8698, which is 0xae103f9f8698 sign-extended from bit 47).

It only fires once the heap has multiple nodes to meld, i.e. under real
pthread-mutex contention: a guest psynch mutex drop -> ksyn_mtxsignal ->
waitq_wakeup64_thread -> waitq_select_thread_locked -> priority_queue_remove ->
meld_pair. A single-threaded guest never hits it; a parallel guest build (many
mutex waiters) crashes the daemon.

Mask the unpacked value to the low CHILD_BITS so the pointer is zero-extended,
correct for every 48-bit userspace address (and a no-op on x86_64 userspace). A
real KERNEL build is unaffected -- this is the ciderd userspace duct-tape.

--- a/libkern/c++/priority_queue.cpp
+++ b/libkern/c++/priority_queue.cpp
@@ -134,7 +134,22 @@
 	static inline entry_t
 	unpack_child(entry_t e)
 	{
+#ifdef __LP64__
+		/*
+		 * `child` is a signed `long :48` bitfield, so reading it sign-extends bit 47.
+		 * Upstream XNU relies on that to rebuild 0xffff... KERNEL pointers (and to keep NULL
+		 * as 0). ciderd, however, runs this scheduler in USERSPACE: on arm64 the user VA is
+		 * 48-bit and ciderd itself is mapped high (e.g. 0xae..._....), so a perfectly valid
+		 * node pointer can have bit 47 set. Sign-extending it yields a bogus 0xffff... address
+		 * and meld_pair() then faults (SIGSEGV) on the first multi-node heap merge -- i.e.
+		 * under real pthread-mutex contention such as a parallel guest build. Mask to the low
+		 * CHILD_BITS so the pointer is zero-extended, which is correct for every 48-bit
+		 * userspace address (bit 47 is 0 on x86_64 userspace, so this is a no-op there).
+		 */
+		return (entry_t)((unsigned long)e->child & ((1UL << PRIORITY_QUEUE_ENTRY_CHILD_BITS) - 1));
+#else
 		return (entry_t)e->child;
+#endif
 	}
 
 private:
